.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_RESUME 3 "2026-07-18" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_resume \- resume event delivery for a registration previously paused by ccol_event_loop_pause
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_event_loop_resume(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function works on an fd only. A call on a registration that comes
from a queue selectable or a ccol_channel selectable returns
ccol_invalid_args. ccol_event_loop_modify has the same restriction.

This function recomputes the combined epoll interest mask of the fd so
that the mask includes reg again. This is an ordinary EPOLL_CTL_MOD
call when the fd is still in the epoll interest set of the kernel.
That is the case when the other direction on the same fd is live and
not paused. It is an EPOLL_CTL_ADD call when ccol_event_loop_pause
removed the fd from that set completely, because every direction was
paused. The direction of reg, its generation and what it adds to
ccol_event_loop_reg_count all stay as they were before the pause. This
call only reverses what ccol_event_loop_pause did to the interest
mask.

The call also arms again a reg that the loop stopped watching because
an event reached it that none of its handlers can take; see
ccol_event_loop_add(3).

A resume of a reg that is neither paused nor stopped in that way
succeeds and does nothing. That covers a reg that nothing ever paused,
and one that something already resumed. This mirrors the "already in the state that you ask
for" behaviour of ccol_event_loop_modify. It is deliberate, and it is
not an error.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  ccol_event_loop the registration belongs to.
reg   Registration to resume.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        The function resumed reg, or reg was not paused.
ccol_invalid_args   loop is CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID, or a handle that
                    is stale, or one that something already destroyed.
                    Or reg is CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID, or it fails to
                    resolve for another reason. Or reg is a
                    registration for a queue or for a ccol_channel. A
                    reg that fails to resolve covers a removal at the
                    same time. For example, something closes the
                    connection while the caller still thinks that it
                    owns a paused registration to resume.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call at the same time as ccol_event_loop_remove(). Also safe to
call from inside a callback that runs on one of the reactor threads.
A caller commonly calls it from a thread other than the one that
paused reg. See the EXAMPLES section of ccol_event_loop_pause(3).
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_pause (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_add (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_remove (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_modify (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_reg_generation (3)
